kosha bridge

  -- a structure that spans a gap and carries load across it.
  -- the fundamental civil engineering artefact — resisting gravity over space.
  -- all bridge types solve the same problem differently:
  --
  --   beam bridge:        simplest. beams span between supports.
  --                       limited span (beams sag under own weight).
  --                       bending: compression on top, tension on bottom.
  --
  --   arch bridge:        load converted to compression along the arch.
  --                       stone and concrete are excellent (strong in compression).
  --                       the arch pushes outward at the base — needs abutments.
  --                       Roman arches: no mortar — pure compression geometry.
  --
  --   suspension bridge:  deck hangs from cables. cables in pure tension.
  --                       steel cable: very strong in tension (ultimate 1500+ MPa).
  --                       longest spans possible. Golden Gate: 1280m.
  --                       towers carry compression. anchorages hold cable ends.
  --
  --   cable-stayed:       cables run directly from tower to deck (not via catenary).
  --                       shorter spans than suspension. less cable needed.
  --
  --   truss bridge:       triangle network of members. each in pure tension or compression.
  --                       triangles are rigid — cannot change shape without member length change.
  --                       Warren, Pratt, Howe trusses — different diagonal arrangements.

